---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const app = appBySlug('postscript')!;
const path = '/apps/postscript';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'apps/postscript');
const desc = description(site, 'apps/postscript');

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Is Postscript worth it without Klaviyo or another email ESP?',
    answer: 'Postscript markets SMS and now an email product. Its email launch page describes cohort onboarding through a waitlist, so confirm access before planning a replacement. An existing email provider can remain while the team validates release messaging, replies and cross-channel cancellation.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How does Postscript pricing work on Shopify?',
    answer: 'Compare a dated quote using eligible subscribers, destinations, message segments, carrier fees, minimum commitments and overages. Include reply labor, implementation and any paired email platform. Model the actual peak promotion separately from an ordinary month; no current merchant budget or supplier price is certified here.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Postscript vs Klaviyo SMS for Shopify?',
    answer: 'Compare both products using the same required email and SMS journeys, events, replies and permission checks. Test current suppression and purchase exits across queued messages, sender ownership and replay deduplication. Verify supported features and matched all-in quotes on the intended plans; a shared interface or coordination tag does not by itself prove correct suppression.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does Postscript handle TCPA compliance for Shopify stores?',
    answer: 'Product tooling does not establish that a store complies with applicable requirements. Confirm the current capture, opt-out and scheduling controls for the intended plan and audience, and validate them with controlled records. Check the applicable requirements with appropriate guidance; no legal compliance assessment or prevented violation is claimed here.',
  },
  {
    question: 'When should a store choose Omnisend SMS over Postscript?',
    answer: 'Choose after testing the required journeys and comparing matched costs, staff effort and cross-channel controls. Validate each channel and purpose permission, current suppression and purchase exits before dispatch. Neither an order-count threshold nor a share of attributed recovery establishes which supplier fits the store.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can Postscript recover abandoned carts on Shopify effectively?',
    answer: 'Evaluate recovery with representative carts and valid SMS permission. Test current purchase exits, opt-outs, queue cancellation and event replay before activation. Use an appropriate comparable control to estimate incremental contribution separately from platform attribution. No vinyl merchant recovery test or winning discount sequence is documented here.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Postscript vs Attentive for mid-market Shopify?',
    answer: 'Compare current supported workflows, onboarding, support coverage and matched contract quotes using the same store requirements. Test reply handling and queue coordination before committing. Store revenue alone does not establish whether a supplier minimum or service model is justified; no current contract pricing is certified here.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the biggest Postscript mistake merchants make?',
    answer: 'A material risk is overlapping recovery from separate systems. Assign one owner per message, verify purpose and channel permission, and recheck purchase state and suppression at dispatch, including queued sends. Test replay deduplication and estimate incremental contribution with an appropriate control rather than treating SMS attribution as lift. No universal timing interval prevents collisions.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="relative overflow-hidden py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/apps" class="text-sm font-bold text-[#9a5b16] hover:text-[#24170f]">← All tool reviews</a>
      <p class="catalog-kicker mt-6">Release messaging and SMS ownership</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">{app.name} review for Shopify stores</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">Postscript is worth evaluating for Shopify release alerts, cart reminders and customer replies. Its current platform also markets email and RCS. This review focuses on who approves a limited-stock release, who handles responses and who stops recovery when stock or purchase state changes; adding SMS needs an operational purpose beyond repeating an email.</p>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-8 text-[#5d3a1d]">Use a limited-run vinyl release, a short chocolate sale and a tea replenishment program as proposed acceptance cases. No merchant deployment, attributed recovery share or prevented violation is documented here. Keep release access, marketing permission and support responsibility separate in each case.</p>
      <div class="mt-9 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-postscript">Klaviyo vs Postscript</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/apps/attentive">Attentive review</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container pb-12">
    <TldrBox
      title="Verdict: Postscript for Shopify"
      picks={[
        { label: 'Channel', tool: 'SMS and email', reason: 'Evaluate release alerts and recovery; confirm email onboarding access.' },
        { label: 'Compliance', tool: 'Core product', reason: 'Validate current capture, opt-out and scheduling controls; tooling does not certify compliance.' },
        { label: 'Pricing', tool: 'Usage-based', reason: 'Request a matched quote including message segments, destinations, fees and labor.' },
        { label: 'Tradeoff', tool: 'Access and handoff', reason: 'Validate email availability and existing queue ownership before consolidation.' },
        { label: 'Best for', tool: 'Mobile-heavy DTC', reason: 'Instagram/TikTok traffic with SMS-opted audience.' },
      ]}
    />

    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Why SMS deserves its own Shopify tool</h2>
      <p>
        Evaluate Postscript against a defined SMS use case rather than assuming texts outperform email. Test the intended account controls with permitted test records: capture source and purpose, opt-out propagation, quiet-hour settings and queued-message cancellation. No blocked sends or legal compliance outcome has been observed for this review. Current product behavior and applicable requirements need separate verification.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a hypothetical $95 tea gift-set abandonment, test whether a permitted SMS adds useful information after the email journey. Recheck purchase state, suppression and stock before dispatch. A vinyl restock alert should correspond to the requested subscription and current inventory. Compare attributed revenue separately from contribution estimated with a suitable control; neither mobile traffic nor urgency guarantees a return.
      </p>
      <p>
        A mechanical keyboard shop can evaluate reply handling with switch-compatibility questions, agent routing, response times and after-hours expectations. Confirm supported account features and integration behavior in each supplier under comparison. Include staff time and escalation work in the cost model rather than assuming one vendor handles conversations better.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="ledger-card mb-12">
      <p class="catalog-kicker">Shopify integration depth</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Native SMS events and email handoff</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-8 text-[#4a2d17]">
        Validate the installed Shopify integration with controlled cart, browse and order records. Check event availability, identification, permission and missing-field behavior before enrollment. Reconcile automation reporting with your own order records and attribution definitions; a synchronized growth tag does not establish permission for every SMS purpose.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-8 text-[#4a2d17]">
        <strong>Standout:</strong> Evaluate the current plan for cart messaging, segmented broadcasts, replies, opt-out handling, reporting and Shopify event support. Document what is demonstrated in your account and what remains unverified; product controls alone are not a compliance determination.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-8 text-[#4a2d17]">
        <strong>Gaps:</strong> Check the full email and SMS requirements against current supported products and plans. Model usage, staff time, integrations and queue coordination before deciding whether a specialist or unified platform fits the store.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-black text-[#24170f]">1. Cart recovery — vinyl drop</h3>
      <p><strong>Scenario:</strong> A hypothetical $78 vinyl cart during a limited pressing sale. <strong>Postscript setup:</strong> Test a recovery sequence with timing chosen for the buying cycle, valid SMS permission and current purchase exits. Evaluate any incentive against margin and a comparable non-incentive cohort.</p>
      <p><strong>Proposed validation:</strong> Reconcile delivered messages and eligible carts with orders under a documented attribution window. Estimate incremental contribution with comparable randomized cohorts where feasible, accounting for stock constraints and overlapping email. No recovery rate or holdout result is claimed.</p>
      <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-black text-[#24170f]">2. Flash sale broadcast — artisan chocolate</h3>
      <p><strong>Scenario:</strong> A proposed 48-hour artisan chocolate sale. <strong>Postscript setup:</strong> Validate the subscribed audience, intended purpose, quiet-hour behavior and frequency settings. Recheck current purchases and suppression before dispatch rather than relying on an arbitrary seven-day exclusion.</p>
      <p><strong>Proposed validation:</strong> Test scheduled-message behavior with controlled records, including timezone uncertainty and opt-outs after enrollment. Measure contribution after discounts, fulfillment and message costs. No revenue per recipient or blocked send has been observed.</p>
      <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-black text-[#24170f]">3. Coordinated stack — tea + Sequenzy email</h3>
      <p><strong>Proposed scenario:</strong> A tea buyer receives email recovery while a permitted SMS reminder is queued. Set timing from the buying cycle; place an order between enrollment and dispatch and confirm the queued text cancels. An email open is an unreliable proxy for purchase or eligibility.</p>
      <p><strong>Acceptance check:</strong> Reconcile the order identifier and cancellation log in both systems. Retry the event and verify no second reminder is created. Compare a coordinated sequence against an appropriate control; no incremental recovery result has been measured.</p>
      <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-black text-[#24170f]">4. Over-texting failure — keyboard launch</h3>
      <p><strong>Scenario:</strong> A proposed keyboard launch with several promotional touches. <strong>Proposed validation:</strong> Monitor delivery, complaints and opt-outs as frequency changes, with appropriate comparison cohorts. Choose a cadence from permission and customer response; no baseline, failure rate or universal monthly cap is established here.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2 mb-12">
      <div class="card"><p class="catalog-kicker">Pros</p><ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-[#4a2d17]">{app.strengths.map((s) => <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">+</span>{s}</li>)}</ul></div>
      <div class="card"><p class="catalog-kicker">Cons</p><ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-[#4a2d17]">{app.limits.map((l) => <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">−</span>{l}</li>)}</ul></div>
    </div>

    <div class="grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2 mb-12">
      <div class="ledger-card">
        <p class="catalog-kicker">Who should buy</p>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-[#4a2d17]">
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">→</span>Mobile-first DTC with SMS-opted audience</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">→</span>Drop and flash-sale urgency driving revenue</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">→</span>Email ESP locked; need specialized SMS layer</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">→</span>Compliance discipline with quiet-hour rules</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="ledger-card">
        <p class="catalog-kicker">Who should skip</p>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-[#4a2d17]">
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">→</span>Stores whose pilot does not support the added SMS cost and coordination work</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">→</span>Need immediate email access that the proposed account cannot provide</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">→</span>Need service coverage beyond the quoted operating agreement</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">→</span>No email ESP and no lifecycle plan — fix email first</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Postscript operating discipline on Shopify</h2>
      <p>
        Estimate SMS incrementality with a control design suited to audience size, stock availability and the buying cycle. Keep other channel exposure comparable and account for uncertainty, returns, discounts and total costs. Different calendar weeks can confound a comparison. Report platform attribution separately from the estimated effect of sending SMS; this review contains no measured lift.
      </p>
      <p>
        Document frequency controls by audience and purpose, including interaction with campaigns and automated recovery. Assign an owner to mechanical-keyboard compatibility replies and define response expectations and escalation. Include reply labor in the cost comparison; a fixed VIP or general-list cap is not evidence of the right cadence.
      </p>
      <p>If an external email platform remains, specify which system owns the recovery queue and which authoritative purchase event cancels it. Tags can carry coordination state, but document update delay and failure behavior. Test an order during a sync outage, then replay it after reconnection; pause dispatch when eligibility cannot be established.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Proposed release and replenishment ownership</h2>
      <p><strong>Proposed vinyl release:</strong> The release manager approves the allocation and send audience; the SMS operator checks current stock and purchase state. Revoke queued reminders when a pressing sells out. Hourly tag refreshes are insufficient evidence that a launch queue is current.</p>
      <p>
        <strong>Artisan chocolate flash sales:</strong> A proposed SMS broadcast and email fallback should each have valid channel and purpose permission. Choose timing for the sale, assign one sender per touch and cancel stale recovery after purchase. Non-SMS status alone does not authorize email.
      </p>
      <p><strong>Proposed tea replenishment:</strong> Email can own product education while SMS serves an explicitly requested reminder. Assign subscription changes, skips and refunds to an authoritative source. Choose audience and economics from the store requirements rather than an AOV cutoff.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card mb-12">
      <p class="catalog-kicker">Compare alternatives</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Postscript vs other Shopify tools</h2>
      <ul class="mt-6 space-y-3 text-lg font-bold">
        <li><a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-postscript">Klaviyo vs Postscript</a></li>
        <li><a class="link" href="/compare/omnisend-vs-postscript">Omnisend vs Postscript</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

<!-- EXPANSION_BLOCK_v2 -->

  <section class="container py-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Postscript in a three-layer Shopify stack</h2>
      <p>Evaluate Postscript where a separately permitted SMS journey could improve the customer experience. Establish current channel support, operational effort and incremental contribution before treating checkout urgency as a reason to add a platform.</p>
      <p>Postscript now offers more than a specialist SMS layer. Its <a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/email">email launch page</a>, checked November 25, 2026, describes an editor, branching flows and email/SMS steps together, with access opening in onboarding cohorts. Confirm account access, integration fields and export support before consolidating. These advertised capabilities are benefits to test, not independently observed performance.</p>
      
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container py-12">
    <div class="ledger-card">
      <p class="catalog-kicker">Evaluation checklist</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Score Postscript on your Shopify store</h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-[17px] leading-8 text-[#4a2d17]">
        
        <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">→</span>Can your team maintain flows monthly without agency dependency?</li>
        
        <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">→</span>Can finance reconcile attribution separately from estimated incremental contribution?</li>
        <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-[#9a5b16]">→</span>Does SMS/email/push suppression integrate or require manual tags?</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

<!-- EXPANSION_BLOCK_v3 -->

  <section class="container py-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Postscript migration and co-existence</h2>
      <p>A migration decision should distinguish replacing the SMS sender from replacing email. Inventory keywords, opt-in sources, release subscriptions, active conversations and undelivered queues. Ask how sender continuity, reply history and withdrawals transfer; retain the existing email program until the new email account passes its own acceptance cases. No merchant savings or migration speed is established.</p>
      <p>Use controlled records or mutually exclusive live cohorts to validate migration. Assign a single owner to each message and reconcile old enrollment and queued sends before cutover. Verify current suppression, purchase exits and stable replay deduplication keys. A fixed parallel-send period or attribution agreement is not a readiness test.</p>
      <p>When combining SMS, capture and shipping tools, document which system owns events, enrollment, queues and dispatch. Confirm each installed product capability rather than inferring it from a vendor name. Test permission scope, opt-outs, current purchase exits and replay behavior across system boundaries.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

<!-- TARGETED_EXPANSION_v4 -->

  <section class="container py-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Two-way SMS for high-consideration carts</h2>
      <p>A hypothetical climbing-harness shop could test reply routing for sizing questions with staff trained to escalate product-safety questions appropriately. Record actual response times, labor and customer outcomes; a conversation conversion comparison needs comparable cohorts. No merchant experiment or conversion rate is documented here.</p>
      <p>Check the requirements applicable to the audience and message purpose with appropriate guidance, then validate supported scheduling and opt-out controls in the intended account. Test timezone uncertainty and queued sends using controlled records. No avoided fine or prevented violation has been demonstrated, and product tooling is not insurance or a legal conclusion.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container py-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">SMS as precision channel, not email duplicate</h2>
      <p>A limited pressing is an allocation problem as well as a messaging problem. Record the release identifier, available units, audience policy, approved offer and stop condition before scheduling. Test a sellout after queue creation and a refund after purchase; neither should silently re-enroll someone in a stale promotion. Compare net contribution after fulfillment, refunds, message fees and support work rather than using a universal minimum cart value.</p>
      <p>
        Compare reply handling with the same climbing-harness support scenario across suppliers. Test routing, staff coverage, escalation and after-hours expectations, and include labor alongside message costs. Confirm current controls and applicable requirements separately; no conversion result or prevented complaint is established here.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Three merchant stacks with Postscript</h2>
      <p><strong>Stack A — external email plus Postscript SMS.</strong> A proposed coffee release keeps education in the existing email provider and requested flash-roast alerts in Postscript. Approve each channel separately, log the campaign identifier and define cancellation on sellout or purchase. Timing and collision rules require validation; no fixed gap guarantees incremental value.</p>
      <p>
        <strong>Stack B — Klaviyo unified SMS.</strong> A hypothetical outdoor-gear store should compare a unified workflow with a separate SMS integration using the same events and suppression tests. Record staff effort, quote scope and failure recovery before choosing; an order-count threshold does not establish the better architecture.
      </p>
      <p><strong>Stack C — existing Shopify mail plus Postscript SMS.</strong> A board-game publisher could keep its current mail setup while testing requested restock alerts. Confirm stock, preorder status and purpose-specific permission, and assign the person who resolves customer replies when allocation fails. Include both platforms and support labor in the quote comparison.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Buyer checklist before Postscript annual commit</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Is permission documented for the intended SMS purpose and audience?</li>
        <li>Are frequency and economic rules justified by customer response and contribution?</li>
        <li>Email lifecycle has an assigned owner, whether inside Postscript or elsewhere?</li>
        <li>Quiet hours and state-specific compliance configured — not default UTC?</li>
        <li>Peak-week SMS budgeted separately from baseline month in finance model?</li>
        <li>Is a suitable control planned for incremental contribution with uncertainty disclosed?</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Choose a platform only after checking its current product scope against the required email and SMS work. Document sender ownership, queue cancellation and collision rules across the stack. See the <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">abandoned cart guide</a> for planning a coordinated recovery sequence.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Peak-week SMS budgeting for finance</h2>
      <p>Model peak SMS usage separately from an ordinary month using eligible recipients, message segments, destinations, carrier charges, minimum commitments, overages and staff time. Request dated matched quotes; no peak or baseline merchant bill is documented here. Use a suitable randomized control to estimate contribution, with uncertainty and inventory constraints disclosed.</p>
      <p>A hypothetical coffee flash-roast alert could target subscribers who requested that purpose. Coordinate email separately using its own permission and current suppression. Monitor sellout, complaints and opt-outs, and compare contribution after discounts and all-in costs; no audience-margin benefit has been observed.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Two-way SMS labor model</h2>
      <p>For a harness-sizing reply workflow, define staff coverage, escalation and after-hours expectations. Record actual reply hours and message fees alongside any outcomes. No conversion rate is documented; include labor and uncertainty in any proposed ROI comparison.</p>
      <p>Validate the current scheduling controls and applicable audience requirements before a promotional send. Test uncertain timezones, opt-outs and queued-message cancellation with controlled records. No prevented violation or complaint spike is documented; operational controls do not establish legal compliance.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Channel priority wiki template</h2>
      <p>Write a channel-priority record for each release: campaign and SKU identifiers, permission source, inventory authority, purchase exit, queue owner, approver, reply rota and stop switch. Include fallback behavior for stale stock or delayed events. Rehearse a sold-out release and a late opt-out before activating; choose timing and cart economics from the actual program.</p>
      <p>For a proposed sizing conversation, document staff coverage and escalation. Keep peak message and reply-labor budgets separate from the ordinary month, and use a suitable control to estimate contribution. No merchant recovery or quarterly incrementality result is claimed.</p>

      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Postscript buyer desk: SMS incrementality proof</h2>
      <p>Plan an incrementality study before making an annual commitment. Choose cohort size and duration for the buying cycle and expected uncertainty; hold email exposure comparable where feasible and account for stock limits and spillovers. Validate capture purpose, channel permission, current suppression, purchase exits, queue ownership and replay deduplication before activation. Compare contribution after discounts, returns, labor and matched message costs. A flat result or a short-term revenue decline alone does not settle platform value.</p>
      <p>A proposed harness-sizing workflow needs appropriately trained reply staff and clear escalation. A board game restock workflow needs current inventory checks and coordinated recovery ownership. Test email follow-up with its own permission and timing suited to customer needs; no recovery benefit or six-hour returns threshold has been observed.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Postscript Shopify FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
